## Series context & the key point _(why this concept, not a generic one)_

Part 4.0 is **"The Margin."** The whole arc up to here was "The Work": Part 1 (what phase to be in), Part 2 (the daily structure), Part 3.1–3.3 (the program — concepts, rules, runnable templates). Part 4 is the deliberate anticlimax. Its opening move is the **90/10 Rule**: *the program is 90% of your results; pharmacology and advanced supplementation are the final 10% — the chemistry that sharpens the edges of work you've already done.* The article is emphatic that **if the Work isn't mastered, the Margin is wasted money.**

The article then splits into **two tiers separated by a hard safety boundary**, and this two-tier split is the structural spine the poster must carry:

1. **Tier 1 — Natty.** Everything legal, OTC, and broadly safe at sensible doses. Organized into **four mechanism buckets**: (a) **Energy / general cardio** — TMG, methylated B12; (b) **Pre-workout cognition** — Alpha-GPC + Huperzine A (the "more produced, less broken down" acetylcholine pairing); (c) **Fat loss, cut-specific** — caffeine, L-carnitine, yohimbine (the alpha-2 antagonist that only works *fasted*); (d) **Pump / recovery / basics** — L-citrulline, creatine monohydrate. This tier is "generally appropriate for almost anyone serious about training."
2. **Tier 2 — Half-Natty.** Peptides and research compounds in the gray zone between supplement and pharmacology — **Retatrutide** (triple GLP-1/GIP/glucagon agonist), **CJC-1295 + Ipamorelin** (GH secretagogue pair), **MK-677** (oral ghrelin mimetic), **Clenbuterol / Cardarine**. The article is unambiguous: **this is NOT a recommendation tier — it's a transparency tier.** The gate into it is three non-negotiables: comprehensive bloodwork, a doctor who knows what's being run, and a structured plan (dose / duration / monitoring).

And braided through the whole piece is the idea a generic "supplement chart" would destroy: **the honest hierarchy of impact, which most people invert.** The article ranks it explicitly — (1) training stimulus & consistency, (2) calorie management, (3) sleep & recovery, (4) Tier 1 supplements, (5) Tier 2 compounds — and then names the failure mode directly: *"People get this hierarchy backward. They optimize the Tier 2 stack while sleeping six hours, training inconsistently, and eating in fluctuating windows."* The compounds are meaningful **only on top of the first four; trivial without them.**

The prior posters used distinct sub-archetypes inside the locked Engineering Diagnostic Instrument style: 1.1/1.2 were **lookup matrices**, 2.0 a **signal-trace day-strip**, 3.1 an **operating-band curve dashboard with a fault annex**, 3.2 a **build-up assembly schematic with a standing-orders rail**, 3.3 a **dual-configuration datasheet with a shared output meter + mesocycle timeline + selector gate.** Part 4.0 needs another distinct-but-cohesive sub-archetype. The cleanest fit for an article that is fundamentally *a small margin layered on a large foundation, then a two-tier catalog split by a hard safety interlock, with an un-invertible priority ladder*: a **gain-stage / margin panel sitting on top of a foundation block, feeding a two-zone gated compound catalog with a physical safety interlock between the open tier and the quarantined tier.** Think of it as the instrument that documents a "trim" or "fine-gain" stage — the panel is honest that it adjusts only the last few percent of a signal whose magnitude was already set upstream.

**The single key point to make a reader feel:** *Everything on this poster is the last 10%. The bar underneath it — program, diet, sleep — is 90%, and it is drawn bigger than every compound here combined. Tier 1 is an open shelf you can use. Tier 2 sits behind a locked interlock you do not open without bloodwork, a doctor, and a plan — and even then it's shown for transparency, not endorsement. The priority ladder people invert is pinned here right-side-up so the order can't be forgotten.* The visual must make the **90/10 proportion** and the **hard gate between the two tiers** the two dominant reads — not the compound list.

